WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
 >> Hello, and welcome to our lecture on Python dictionaries.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.400
 So Python dictionaries are an example of a collection.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:13.000
 And a collection is just a way for us to put more than one value in a variable and sort

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.400
 of take a group of values that are usually similar in one way or the other, but move

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.400
 them around together.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.880
 It's kind of like putting things in a piece of luggage and carrying them all around together.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.300
 Each of the things is distinct, but we can carry them around together.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.440
 And so it's just nice and convenient, but we do have to organize them.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.860
 So a good example of a disorganized collection is the desktop that I'm sitting at in front

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.440
 of right now as I'm making this lecture.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.720
 And you can see that's just very disorganized.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.680
 And so part of the goal of a collection is organizing it.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.760
 Now what's not a collection?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.720
 A symbol variable is not a collection.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.200
 So we put x equals 2, 2 is an x.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.540
 One statement later we say x equals 4, and 4 is an x, but 2 is long gone.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.820
 So we can't put two things in x at the same time.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.600
 But if x is a collection, we can put two things in at the same time.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.960
 Now we've already covered lists.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.800
 A list is a linear collection of values, and you can look it up by position, somewhere

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.740
 between 0 and the length of that list minus 1.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.440
 But a dictionary is a collection of key-value pairs.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.540
 You can look them up by tag or key.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.800
 Now it turns out that dictionaries also have an order.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.400
 So a filing cabinet with a bunch of tabs is a good example.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.940
 If you have a bunch of books in a bookshelf, and you can read the names of the books.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.740
 The content is the book itself, but you've got the titles on the back of books.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.440
 Another thing that you might imagine is I bet we all have like a box or a drawer that

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.840
 has a bunch of computer cables in it, and I did too.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.320
 And I used to just throw extra computer cables in, but I had so many extra computer cables

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.360
 so I came up with a new trick.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:01.200
 The new trick is to put all the cables of a certain type into the same bag.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.720
 And then I would put a label on, and that's the key essence of key-value pairs.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.720
 The key in this is HDMI.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.420
 The value is what's inside of it.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:17.640
 So I should be able to go into my junk drawer and say, "Hey, give me my HDMI cables."

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.640
 And I would get them back.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.620
 Now Python's collection is very efficient at getting it back.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:27.360
 So I say like, "Hey, get my firewire cables," and this would just come out because this

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.520
 is my firewire bag, my bag of firewire cables.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.920
 That's how long I've been doing video is I have a bag of firewire cables that I don't

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.900
 use very often.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:45.160
 Now the collection, whether it's a bookshelf or a drawer full of bags of cables that are

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.820
 marked has a key value, but it also then has an order.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.140
 But the key thing is that in Python, unlike in the real world, the best way to look things

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:58.160
 up is by the tag or the key, not going all the way through them.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.060
 So dictionaries are Python's most powerful data collection, and they allow us to do fast

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.280
 database-like operations in Python.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.700
 There are similar concepts in many different programming languages that you may or may

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:16.840
 not have heard of, like associative arrays, like Perl or PHP, properties, property maps

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:26.440
 or hash maps or tree maps and Java or property bags, and C++ also has a map concept.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.100
 Now dictionaries have changed over time in Python.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:35.240
 Prior to 2018, dictionaries did not keep entries in their order of insertion.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.840
 You'd put them in and they kind of get all jumbled up and randomized, and that's because

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.560
 the underlying storage mechanism, what's called a hash table, purely hash table, which does

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.320
 not keep things in order.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:52.880
 And since 2018, Python 3.7, the dictionaries keep entries in the order that they were inserted.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.240
 Now they're not sorted, not sorted by key, not sorted by value, and one of the things

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.140
 we're going to do is do a lot of sorting by key and value.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.300
 They're stored in insertion order.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:09.980
 And so it's almost like dictionaries after Python 3.7 are kind of like lists but with

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.660
 a very different way of storing and looking things up.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:19.260
 So you store them, they kind of stay in order, but in lists you use numbers to get them out

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.440
 and in Python you use whatever the keys are that you've done.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:31.120
 Now Python lists and dictionaries are elegant, abstract, beautiful, easy to use objects,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.040
 and that's part of the reason people like using Python is because things like lists

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.280
 and dictionaries just work so well.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.800
 For now, let's just pretend they're magical.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.880
 We're going to understand them and be thankful that Guido and the others who built Python

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.720
 for us just made them well-designed and easy for us to use.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.220
 But underneath the covers, the code to support them is actually pretty complex.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:01.680
 The way that computer scientists built the implementation for dictionaries in lists are

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.000
 things like dynamic memory, arrays, linked lists, hash maps, and trees.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.720
 And those are all computer science concepts that for now I might mention the word hash

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:18.140
 in passing, but in general that implementation detail is for a later course, and it's most

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:24.600
 commonly a course that we do in C. So we can go from easy, super easy to use in Python,

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.520
 to like, "Whoa, this is hard to build."

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.200
 That's actually part of the skill.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.600
 But for now, just ignore this.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.320
 So let's take a quick review of how lists work.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.740
 So we append values to the end of the list, it's the only thing we can do, and we look

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.280
 at them by position.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.040
 We can change things, but if we want to insert something then they all got to be shuffled

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.760
 down a little bit.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:51.040
 So we're going to call cards equals list, open print, closed print, which is the list constructor.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.880
 We're going to append in order, 12, 3, and 75.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.000
 And then we're going to print them out, and we get them out with square brackets, 12, 3,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.240
 and 75.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.080
 We can look them up by position.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:08.640
 Cards sub 1, so they start at 0, so that's actually the second one, and that value there

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.640
 is 3.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:15.440
 So in the second position in the list, which is the sub 1 position in the list, the value

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.560
 is 3.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:23.320
 We can use this sort of index syntax of square brackets, cards sub 1 plus 2 on the right

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.760
 hand side of an assignment statement.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.640
 So that'll retrieve the value 3, add 2 to it, and then store it back in the cards sub

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:31.640
 1.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:37.400
 And so we can, if we're using the index expression, we can treat that kind of like a variable.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:44.480
 And if we print cards afterwards, we see that the second value has moved from 3 to 5.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:50.120
 Doing a similar kind of thing in dictionaries, we make a thing called cabinet, and we call

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:54.840
 the dictionary constructor, and we say cabinet sub summer equals 12.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:07:02.560
 So just like in my drawer of cables, I had to pick a label before I threw it in.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.840
 And so in this case, summer is the label and 12 is the value.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.800
 Now, they can be much larger than that.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.320
 I'm using keys as strings and integers as values because it's a very common use case,

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.200
 but they can be much more complex than that.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.920
 Cabinet sub fall equals 3 and cabinet sub spring equals 75.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.160
 They're being inserted.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.080
 So when you print them, you see summer 12, fall 3, spring 75.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:31.500
 But the thing that's key about this is you might put a million things in there, but printing

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.880
 cabinets of fall does not need to read all million things.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.560
 A list, you might have to read all million things to find something.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.560
 But dictionaries and how they're built using hash maps, how they're built, cabinet sub

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.160
 fall is in a sense guaranteed to you to be instant, virtually instantaneous.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.400
 So we, even though there's only three in this one, if there's a million, it would be just

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.400
 about as fast.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.740
 And so we get that three.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:01.360
 We can also use this index syntax with a string as index, pull off the old version, which

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.460
 is 3, add 2 to it, becomes 5, goes back in, the cabinets of fall, and you print it.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:15.440
 And you can see that the entire cabinet is unchanged except the key fall is now 5.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:18.140
 So you can kind of see this, right?

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:21.680
 The only real difference is how we index these things.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.740
 If we look at them, they kind of accumulate values in order, but in dictionaries we're

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:32.220
 accumulating keys and values, and then the way we look things up is by key in dictionaries

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.800
 and by position in values.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:43.640
 And we can both look things up and we can change them in both dictionaries and lists.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:48.320
 Like anything, you've got to have a way to put a constant because you want to say a set

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.840
 of key value pairs, so it's really simple.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.460
 The constant looks exactly like the printout.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:57.800
 So if you print a dictionary, it has a curly brace and then a key colon value comma key

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.460
 colon value comma key colon value.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:06.640
 And so you can do that, and that's a literal, that's a dictionary constant, sign that into

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.720
 JJJ, and then you print that out and you get back basically the same thing.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:17.180
 And if you just do curly brace curly brace, you get an empty dictionary.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:22.760
 So up next, we're going to use dictionaries to go through a seemingly unlimited length

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:27.840
 list of names or eventually words, and we're going to use dictionaries to accumulate a

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:34.480
 histogram of the frequency of names in a list of names, and then look and find the most

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:34.960
 common name.

